# keur quality control — directory holding too many logical units.
#
# Why this exists: keur's norm (AGENTS.md) is "no dir > ~10 files; regroup into
# subdirs." This was the directory half of an external shell lint
# (scripts/check-basics.sh, now retired). It is a CROSS-FILE AGGREGATION (count
# units per directory), which a per-line rule cannot express — but the FILE-LEVEL
# engine layer can, because it sees the whole fileset at once (the same layer as
# file-absence/file-presence). So this norm is now a real keur rule too.
#
# HOW: the `max-dir-files` engine groups matched files by their parent directory
# and counts logical units — a `.h` + `.cpp` with the same stem count as ONE
# module — flagging a directory with more than min_count units, once per dir.
#
# WARN, not block: directory size is an organisation smell, not a bug. It nudges
# toward regrouping into subdirectories. Tests/vendor/build excluded.
#
# @see docs/rules-authoring.md (`max-dir-files`), AGENTS.md.
id: KEUR-QUAL-006
ilities: [analysability]
maturity: 3
title: Directory holds more than ~10 logical units (regroup into subdirs)
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: max-dir-files
action: warn
min_count: 10
target:
  extensions: .c .cpp .cc .h .hpp
  exclude_paths: test/ tests/ _test.cpp vendor/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  - regex: too many units
    message: "Directory holds more than ~10 logical units (a .h/.cpp pair counts as one). Regroup into subdirectories by responsibility, the way eval/ or cmd/ are organised. @see KEUR-QUAL-006, AGENTS.md"
confidence: 0.6
fix: "Split the directory into responsibility-named subdirectories (e.g. learn/, manager/, config/) so each holds a cohesive set of modules under the ~10-unit norm. A .h/.cpp pair is one unit — count modules, not raw files."
